{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Let's find two pairs of values in order to graph the function.\n\n$\\begin{align}\ng(\\blueD 0)\u0026=\\dfrac12\\cdot2^{\\blueD 0}=\\dfrac12\n\\\\\\\\\ng(\\blueD 1)\u0026=\\dfrac12\\cdot2^{\\blueD 1}=1\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"**Now we graph the function using the points $\\left(0,\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $(1,1)$.**\n\n[[☃ grapher 3]]\n\n[[☃ explanation 2]]","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{"explanation 2":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"explanation":"The horizontal line in the widget is called an *asymptote*. This is a line that a function gets close to, but never touches, for very large positive or negative values of $x$.\n\nIn this case, our exponential function gets close to, but never reaches $0$ for large negative values of $x$. So the asymptote is the line $y=0$, and we should leave the asymptote on the $x$-axis.","hidePrompt":"Hide explanation","showPrompt":"What is the horizontal line?","static":false,"widgets":{}},"static":false,"type":"explanation","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}},"grapher 3":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"availableTypes":["exponential"],"correct":{"asymptote":[[-5,0],[5,0]],"coords":[[0,0.5],[1,1]],"type":"exponential"},"graph":{"backgroundImage":{"url":null},"editableSettings":["graph","snap","image"],"gridStep":[0.5,0.5],"labels":["x","y"],"markings":"graph","range":[[-6,6],[-6,6]],"rulerLabel":"","rulerTicks":10,"showProtractor":false,"showRuler":false,"showTooltips":false,"snapStep":[0.5,0.5],"step":[1,1],"valid":true}},"static":true,"type":"grapher","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Graph the function.**\n\n$g(x)=\\dfrac12\\cdot2^x$\n\n[[☃ grapher 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"grapher 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"availableTypes":["exponenti